# NAIGX production environment — copy to `deploy/.env` and fill in.
#
#   cp deploy/.env.example deploy/.env
#
# ⚠️ `deploy/.env` IS GITIGNORED AND THIS FILE IS NOT. Never put a real
# secret in `.env.example`.
#
# Every variable below without a default is enforced: compose refuses to start
# without it, or `loadConfig` refuses to boot. That is deliberate — each one is
# a value whose absence would otherwise be papered over by a development
# default that is wrong in production.

# --- Edge -----------------------------------------------------------------

# The public hostname. Its DNS A/AAAA record must already point at this host
# BEFORE the first `up`, because Caddy requests a certificate immediately and a
# challenge that cannot be reached counts against the Let's Encrypt rate limit.
NAIGX_DOMAIN="naigx.example.com"

# Where Let's Encrypt sends renewal-failure notices. Use an address that is
# actually read; the first symptom of a silently failing renewal is an expired
# certificate.
ACME_EMAIL="you@example.com"

# --- Database -------------------------------------------------------------

POSTGRES_USER="naigx"

# Generate, do not invent:  openssl rand -base64 32
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=""

POSTGRES_DB="naigx"

# --- Application ----------------------------------------------------------

# Salt for the stored session and audit IP hash (`DB §4.1`, `DB §13`).
# The application REFUSES TO START under NODE_ENV=production without it: the
# development default is a constant in this repository, and an IP hash salted
# with a public constant enumerates back to the address in seconds, which makes
# the "Pseudonymous" classification false.
#
# Generate:  openssl rand -hex 32
#
# ⚠️ CHANGING THIS INVALIDATES EXISTING `ip_hash` COMPARISONS. Old rows do not
# become readable, they become uncomparable — abuse detection (`NFR-025`) loses
# its history. Rotate deliberately, not casually.
NAIGX_IP_HASH_SECRET=""

# The operator credential for `/internal/*` (D-48). Absent means the internal
# surface is DISABLED, which is a valid posture — Phase 4 needs it set so
# metrics can be scraped. `/internal/*` is blocked at the edge either way.
#
# Generate:  openssl rand -hex 32
NAIGX_OPERATOR_TOKEN=""

LOG_LEVEL="info"

# --- Not set here, deliberately -------------------------------------------
#
# NAIGX_EXECUTION_MODE — unset resolves to replay, which bills nothing. The
#   standing no-provider-spend constraint means this file is not where a
#   metered mode gets switched on.
#
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY / PROVIDER_MODEL / PROVIDER_*_USD_PER_MTOK — only needed
#   for a metered instance. See `backend/.env.example`.
#
# HOST — stays at its `0.0.0.0` default. Inside a container that means "every
#   interface of this container", which is what Caddy needs; the app is kept
#   off the internet by publishing no port for it, not by the bind address.

# --- Managed key service (D-52, DB §13.1 row 3) -----------------------------
#
# ⚠️ REQUIRED. The application refuses to start under NODE_ENV=production
# without these, and there is deliberately NO local-key fallback: D-52 §4
# rejects a locally-held key as "not a cheaper version of this decision, it is
# the absence of it" — every free option stores the key on the machine that
# holds the storage, buying none of the property the layer exists for.
#
# ⚠️ LOSING THE CMK DESTROYS raw_content, structured_input AND
# structured_output — in the database AND in every backup (D-52 §6). Enable
# deletion protection on the key. Its existence is part of the backup story.
#
# Cost: $1/month per key, 20,000 free requests/month — re-verified against the
# official pricing page on 2026-09-08. That is an estimate on its date, not a
# guarantee: read the page again before creating the key. This design makes one
# request per process start, so usage does not grow with analysis volume.
NAIGX_KMS_KEY_ID="alias/naigx"
NAIGX_KMS_REGION="ap-southeast-1"

# AWS credentials are NOT set here. They come from the ambient provider chain —
# an instance role, or the environment the container is given (`SA §10.4`:
# injected at runtime, never in source, images, or configuration files).
